<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chalukya dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chalukyas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chalukya dynasty ruled large parts of southern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> between the 6th and 12th centuries. The Chalukyas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-i">Pulakeshin I</a> around 543, dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> until the Rashtrakutas overthrew them in 753; the Western Chalukyas of Kalyani revived their power from 973 to about 1189, while an Eastern Chalukya line ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a>. Their temples at Badami, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a> are landmarks of Indian architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty.jpg" alt="Chalukya dynasty" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan. Reference: [1] Borders by Rawn3012 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chalukyas_at_their_height.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
